---
/** 유효성 평가 허브. ★ 보고서가 있는 도구와 없는 도구를 **함께** 든다 —
 *  있는 것만 보이면 쪽이 「전부 평가됐다」고 읽히고, 그것은 없는 것을 주장하는 일이다. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u, SITE_NAME } from "../../lib/site";
import { TOOLS } from "../../lib/tools";
import { REPORTS, reportFor } from "../../validation/registry";
import { UNIT_CASES } from "../../validation/units.cases";
/** ★ 건수는 **세어서** 그린다 — 등록부에 글자로 적으면 단위가 늘 때 그 줄만 낡는다. */
const unitsStats = (() => {
  const byQ = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const c of UNIT_CASES) byQ.set(c.quantity, (byQ.get(c.quantity) ?? 0) + 1);
  let pairs = 0;
  for (const n of byQ.values()) pairs += n * (n - 1);
  return { units: UNIT_CASES.length, pairs };
})();
import ToolIcon from "../../components/ToolIcon.astro";

const published = REPORTS.filter((r) => r.status === "published").length;
const rows = TOOLS.map((t) => ({ tool: t, report: reportFor(t.slug) }));

const title = "Validation — how the results on this site are checked";
const description =
  "Hand calculations set against what each calculator returns, with the sources used, the acceptance criterion and the coverage. One report per tool.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema="CollectionPage">
  <nav class="tap-list mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-1.5 text-[12px] text-ink-faint">
    <a href={u("/")} class="hover:text-ink">Calculators</a> <span aria-hidden>/</span> Validation
  </nav>

  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">Validation</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    Whether a calculator can be trusted is decided by what stands behind its answer, not by the answer
    itself. These reports work each calculation by hand from a published definition and set the result
    against what the tool returns, with every step shown.
  </p>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-8 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2>What a report contains</h2>
    <p>
      Each report states what it covers, derives the expected values from primary sources, names the
      acceptance criterion, shows the working in full, and ends with what the exercise does not
      establish. The expected values are held apart from the calculation engine and are written from
      the sources rather than computed from the tool's own constants, because a tool compared against
      itself will always agree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coverage is counted rather than asserted. A report states how many of the things the tool offers
      it has actually derived, the build compares that count against the tool, and a mismatch fails the
      build. Adding a capability without validating it is therefore not possible.
    </p>

    <h2>Reports</h2>
    <p>
      {published} of {TOOLS.length} calculators has a published report. The rest are listed so that the
      gap is visible; the work is proceeding one tool at a time and this page is the record of where it
      has reached.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-3xl gap-3">
    {rows.map(({ tool, report }) => (
      <li class="card p-4">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <ToolIcon slug={tool.slug} class="mt-[3px]" />
          <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <p class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink">{tool.name}</p>
            {report?.status === "published" ? (
              <>
                <p class="mt-1 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{report.summary}</p>
                <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-ink-faint">
                  {tool.slug === "units"
                    ? `${unitsStats.units} units · ${unitsStats.pairs} ordered conversions · `
                    : ""}Report dated {report.validatedOn} ·
                  <a href={u(`/validation/${tool.slug}/`)} class="ml-1 underline hover:text-ink">
                    Read the validation report
                  </a>
                </p>
              </>
            ) : (
              <p class="mt-1 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                No report yet. The calculator is covered by the automated tests described on the
                <a href={u("/methods/")} class="mx-1 underline hover:text-ink">methods page</a>
                but its working has not been written out for reading.
              </p>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-10 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2>Scope of the exercise</h2>
    <p>
      Validating a conversion is a different task from validating a physical model. The unit converter
      rests entirely on definitions, so its report can be complete: there is a finite list of units and
      every one of them is derived. The calculators that use nuclear data and physical models cannot be
      complete in that sense, and their reports will have to say what was compared, against which
      published values, and how far apart the two were. Where a figure on this site is a computed
      derivation rather than a copied table value, the derivation is described on the
      <a href={u("/methods/")}>methods page</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this makes {SITE_NAME} a substitute for an accredited calibration or test, and the
      <a href={u("/disclaimer/")}>disclaimer</a> says so in the terms that matter.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
